{
  "case_id": "2002-02-69",
  "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: How about Rule 73(b)?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When parties knowingly and voluntarily proceed to trial before a magistrate judge, they consent within the meaning of 28 U.S.C. section 636(c)(1)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Do you agree that the consent has to precede the action of the magistrate?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Eskow",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Certainly, our view is that because Congress specified the requirements for local rules in a separate subsection than the consent requirement itself, it was anticipating that there would be some need to protect the voluntariness, but that it is the voluntariness that's the cornerstone, and that it would be a matter of administrative discretion on the local level."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So Congress basically talked about this discussion process against the backdrop of a consent form that had already been communicated to the clerk."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Frost",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "All parties must expressly consent before a magistrate may exercise jurisdiction under section 636(c). First and foremost, this is because the text of the Magistrates Act and Rule 73(b) require express consent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Frost",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The word, express, is not there, Your Honor. It is in Rule 73(b), and in addition, I'd like to point Your Honor -- Your Honor to some of the provisions of 636(c) which strongly indicate that the consent must be expressed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Why not? If -- Justice Kennedy brought up the analogy to personal jurisdiction. If one makes a general appearance just by showing up in court, then any question of whether the court would otherwise have jurisdiction is gone, because there is jurisdiction, personal jurisdiction by consent, just by making a general appearance, so why isn't showing up in that magistrate's courtroom, going to trial without objecting, why isn't that equivalent to a general appearance?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Title 28, 1653 reads, defective allegations of jurisdiction may be amended upon terms in the trial appellate court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But may I ask, does he even have the authority to say, let's go, before consent has been given?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2002-02-69",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68c1690f074181cc8644817f744cd23cd5564496b905f23e8d16205d2efeb51d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68c1690f0741-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Consent must be something you communicate to the clerk. It must be something clear, and that is why eight courts of appeals, we respectfully submit, have already reached the conclusion there must be express consent."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
